    # on a real machine gets away with them (bash's unwind-protect cleanups,
    # old K&R code). binaryen's --fpcast-emu makes every indirect call and
    # every table entry one uniform signature. gex calls the two entries
    # (__gex_start, __gex_thread_start) with their own signatures, so their
    # table entries go back to the real functions afterwards.
    # every indirect call passes binaryen's maximum number of parameters
    # (16 by default); a program with a wider function (GTK's marshallers
    # take 19) asks for the width it needs
    # A program linked -rdynamic gives dlsym its functions (GtkBuilder's
    # signal handlers): each export gets a thunk too, exported for the
    # loader (gex-dylink fpcast-prep/-post)
    thunks = export_dynamic and os.path.exists(DYLINK)
